{
  "image_name": "6d40c2f154cf13c4d585d6862d148d81.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes the structure and operation of spin-orbit-torque magnetic RAM (SOT‑MRAM), from a single device to an array. At the device level, a magnetic tunnel junction sits on top of an SOT wire. Reading and writing use separate current paths: the read path goes vertically through the junction, while the write path runs laterally through the underlying wire. This separation is the central idea of the figure, because it allows the magnetic state to be switched by spin current generated in the SOT layer without forcing the write current through the tunnel barrier.\n\nA cell schematic shows how this element is connected to read and write word lines, a source line, a bit line, and local interconnects. The larger array view repeats this cell in a grid, indicating how many SOT‑MRAM cells can share read/write lines and source/bit lines in a memory architecture.\n\nThe lower sketches illustrate the switching mechanism: charge current in the SOT wire creates spins that act on the free magnetic layer, with the required current depending on effective fields such as anisotropy and an assist field. The final panel suggests a multi-bit or shared-write-line layout using several magnetic pillars over one write structure.",
